{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Let your foot be rare in your fellow's house, lest he have his fill of you and hate you (Proverbs 25:17).</b>" "<b>Solomon,</b> peace be upon him, teaches us in this verse (Proverbs 25) a lesson in conduct and manners — that a person should strive to acquire friends and pursue their company, and sometimes seek their presence to go to their homes — but not persist in this constantly, for it is this very excess of constancy that he discourages, entering their home all day long. It appears that his intent is that the middle path in this matter is best, for the company of friends when they meet and gather in one place brings benefit: through fellowship love increases and the bond is strengthened and peace endures among them. And thus Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 24): ״איש רעים להתרועע״ — a man of friends to be praised — and there is a friend who clings more than a brother. Scripture says that a person who has the virtue of knowing how to gather friends and companions deserves to be praised by people. And the meaning of להתרועע is 'to be praised,' from the language of (Psalms 150:5): ״בצלצלי תרועה״. For a man of friends surely possesses two virtues: the first, humility — for with arrogance he would not gather them; rather they would flee from him; and the second, that he does not fear people. And so he is saved from two great harms that come from the traits of poverty and wealth, which is what it says (Proverbs 18:23): ״תחנונים ידבר רש ועשיר יענה עזות״ — 'the poor man speaks supplications and the rich man answers with arrogance.' For from poverty comes the harm that the poor man must fear people, and from wealth comes the harm — the trait of arrogance that his wealth bestows upon him. Therefore Scripture mentioned just above this, speaking of the traits of poverty and wealth, as it says: ״תחנונים ידבר רש ועשיר יענה עזות״. Solomon, peace be upon him, intended here to disparage both poverty and wealth, in the same vein as what he said elsewhere (Proverbs 30:8): ״ראש ועשר אל תתן לי״ — 'neither poverty nor riches give me' — and he gave the reason for his words: ״פן אשבע וכחשתי״ — lest I be sated and deny, ״ופן אורש וגנבתי״ — and lest I be impoverished and steal. And so the sage said: the multiplication of wealth harms the soul like the multiplication of blood harms the body. And the explanation of the verse is that the poor man must speak supplications because he fears people, and the rich man — his wealth brings him to the trait of arrogance, to answer with insolence. And immediately Scripture appends to it: ״איש רעים להתרועע״, to explain that a man of friends is saved from these two harms: for since he is a man of friends, he does not fear people, for his friends strengthen his words; and since he is a man of friends, he does not possess the trait of arrogance, for with it he would not have gathered friends, but only with humility. And such a person is worthy of all praise and virtue, for he has in hand the benefits that come from poverty and wealth, while of the harms that come from them he has no share. From all this we learn that from the fellowship of friends great benefits come to a person." "<b>However,</b> Solomon, peace be upon him, warns here that although the fellowship of friends is good and their benefit is great, a person should make his foot rare from entering his fellow's house and not persist so much in coming to his home constantly — for a little is fine, but constancy and excess cause harm, for it turns upon one's fellow as a burden and a bother, until he has his fill of him and hates him. It is like the eating of honey, whose small amount is good and whose excess is harmful — so too the fellowship of friends in entering their homes. Therefore he warned in the verse just before it (Proverbs 25:16): ״דבש מצאת אכול דייך פן תשבענו והקאותו״ — 'honey you found, eat your fill, lest you be sated by it and vomit it' — in order to compare the fellowship of friends to the eating of honey. And the juxtaposition of the verses follows accordingly: just as eating honey, a little is good for it is pleasant and sweet to the body, but excess is harmful until it makes one vomit — so too your fellow's house: entering a little is good, but excess is harmful, for he will have his fill of you until he hates you and casts you away from him. And so excess in all traits brings no benefit — for rain is the life of the world, yet its excess causes harm. And the explanation of the verse 'let your foot be rare from your fellow's house' — it did not say 'from the house of the fellow (הרע),' for then there would be no need to say it regarding an inferior or average person. Rather, even from that honorable, distinguished one who is your fellow (רעך), tested by you and established in great love — you should distance yourself and make your foot rare from entering his home constantly during hours that are not appropriate, such as at the time of eating and drinking, or the time of sleeping, or the time of tending to other needs. And he gave the reason for this: ״פן ישבעך ושנאך״ — lest he have his fill of you and hate you — that is, so that he not have his fill of you, for if you impose upon him by constantly entering his home during these hours, he will surely have his fill of you and hate you, and then the hatred will overpower the love with which he loved you at first, as it is written regarding Amnon (II Samuel 13:15): ״כי גדולה השנאה אשר שנאה מהאהבה אשר אהבה״ — 'for the hatred with which he hated her was greater than the love with which he loved her.'" "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'Let your foot be rare from your fellow's house' — it is written ״הוקר רגלך מבית רעך״, and it is written (Psalms 66:13): ״אבא ביתך בעולות״ — 'I will come to Your house with burnt offerings.' There is no contradiction: here it speaks of sin-offerings and guilt-offerings; there it speaks of burnt offerings and peace offerings. Thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound in the first chapter of Ḥagigah. And the explanation of this Midrash is that it calls the Temple 'the house of your fellow,' and so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded regarding Israel, who are called 'fellows' (ריעים) to the Omnipresent, as it is said (Psalms 122:8): ״למען אחי ורעי״; and so Solomon, peace be upon him, said regarding the Holy One, blessed be He (Proverbs 27:10): ״רעך ורע אביך אל תעזוב״, and it says (Song of Songs 5:16): ״זה דודי וזה רעי״. And the intent of this is to warn a person not to sin and not to persist in coming to the Temple with sin-offerings and guilt-offerings — for a sin-offering and a guilt-offering are sacrifices that come for a sin of deed, and the Holy One, blessed be He, desires more that a person not sin at all and not bring a sacrifice, than that he sin and bring a sacrifice. And so the prophet said (I Samuel 15:22): ״הנה שמוע מזבח טוב״ — 'behold, to obey is better than sacrifice.' Therefore it says: 'let your foot be rare from your fellow's house' — do not act lightly with yourself and multiply transgressions and sins all day and then go to the Temple to offer a sacrifice there, lest the Holy One, blessed be He, have His fill of you and hate you, for He hates those sacrifices, for regarding this it is said (Proverbs 21:27): ״זבח רשעים תועבה״ — 'the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination' — for the wicked man, at the time he brings his sacrifice, his intent turns back to sin, and therefore it is an abomination, for the Holy One, blessed be He, abominates it and hates it. But regarding burnt offerings and peace offerings, David said (Psalms 66:13): ״אבא ביתך בעולות״ — for the burnt offering does not come for a sin of deed, but only for the sin of thought and meditation, from which no person is saved. And it is well known that meditation occurs more at night than during the day, and therefore the Torah fixed the time of the burnt offering at night, so that the atonement be at the time of the transgression. And this is what is written:" "<b>Command Aaron and his sons, saying: This is the law of the burnt offering — it is the burnt offering that is on the fire upon the altar all night until morning, and the fire of the altar shall be kept burning on it.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), this section of the burnt offering encompasses four matters. <b>The first</b> comes to teach us that the burnt-offering sacrifice is the most distinguished of all the sacrifices, and the benefit of its atonement is mighty. <b>The second</b> comes to make known the greatness of the obligation of the created being to worship his Creator. <b>The third</b> is the wondrous sign and great miracle performed upon this altar. And <b>the fourth</b> is the magnitude of the punishment of the wicked who do not want atonement, for they are punished with the torments of Gehenna that have no end or limit, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Gehenna is consumed, but they are not consumed.<br><b>Know that</b> all the types of sacrifices are thirteen, corresponding to the number of the thirteen attributes of mercy. Five of them come from flour, and they are: the meal-offering of fine flour, the meal-offering of the griddle, the meal-offering of the pan, loaves, and wafers — and all of them are called קרבן. The eight of them are from living creatures, and they are: burnt offering, sin offering, guilt offering, thanksgiving offering, peace offerings, firstborn, tithe, and Passover. Some of them are eaten by the priests, such as sin offering, guilt offering, and firstborn; and some are eaten by the owners, such as tithe, Passover, and thanksgiving. The thanksgiving offering comes for a miracle, and it is an expression of acknowledgement — if one was sick and was healed, he brings a thanksgiving offering, as it is written (Psalms 107:22): ״ויזבחו זבחי תודה ויספרו מעשיו ברנה״. Or for other joys, such as the joy of bridegroom and bride, as it is said (Jeremiah 33:11): ״קול ששון וקול שמחה״ … ״מביאים תודה בית ה׳״. And there is one that is eaten neither by the priests nor by the owners, and that is the burnt-offering sacrifice, in which human beings have no share at all — rather it is entirely for the Holy One, blessed be He. This is the meaning of כליל (entirely consumed). And this burnt-offering sacrifice is the most distinguished of all the sacrifices, as the language itself proves, for it says ״היא העולה״ — 'it is the burnt offering' — adding the word היא to indicate the elevation and importance of the sacrifice, and likewise (Ezekiel 10:15): ״היא החיה אשר ראיתי״. Also, its fruit is great in that it atones for the meditation of the heart, as it said regarding Job (Job 1:5): ״והעלה עולות מספר כלם כי אמר איוב אולי חטאו בני וברכו אלהים בלבבם״. And this is the meaning of עולה — that it atones for what rises upon the spirit. And the burnt-offering sacrifice was offered all night until morning, because a person meditates at night and sins by day, as the prophet said (Micah 2:1): ״חושבי און ופועלי רע על משכבותם באור הבקר יעשוה״ — 'they devise iniquity and work evil upon their beds; at the light of dawn they do it.' Therefore the Torah decreed that the atonement be at the time of the transgression. And since the essence of the transgression is in the spirit, everything was entirely consumed in fire and returned to the spirit, and no physical being was permitted to derive benefit from it. This is one matter.<br><b>The second matter:</b> the greatness of the obligation of the created being to worship his Creator. It is well known that a person must be submissive and lowly before the Holy One, blessed be He, when he stands in prayer before Him or performs a commandment or any of the other forms of worship, whether lenient or stringent — that he should adorn them for the honor of the Name, blessed be He, and not spare his own honor, but rather recognize concerning himself that flesh and blood is impoverished and his hand does not reach the mighty obligation incumbent upon him, and it is fitting for him to submit and humble himself, as it is written regarding Abraham (Genesis 18:27): ״ואנכי עפר ואפר״, and King David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 15:4): ״נבזה בעיניו נמאס״ — 'despised in his eyes is [the worthless].' And this is what is said here:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:55.026468120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}